shakes:
Dat ol' mare o' mine?
Yes, huh coat ah long an' shaggy, an' she ain't no shakes to see;
Dat's a ring-bone, yes, you right, suh, an' she got a on'ry knee,

shattered:
And tore all her dresses and ripped her socks;
And shattered the windows with fair-sized rocks;
The words on the fences she'd memorize;

Wanting the sticky, salty sweetness
Of the strong wind and shattered spray;
Wanting the loud sound and the soft sound

shades:
What phantoms fill the dimly lighted room;
What ghostly shades in awe-creating guise
Are bodied forth within the teeming gloom.

shawls:
How cold it is!  Even the lights are cold;
They have put shawls of fog around them, see!
What if the air should grow so dimly white

shamed:
The burden of what those were singing of.
One shamed herself in love; one temperately
Grew gross in soulless love, a sluggish wife;

shaken:
Some instinct pricked my soul to sight;
My heart by some vague thrill was shaken,--
A thrill so true and yet so slight,

shadowy:
Like rain it softly falls at that dim hour
When ghostly lanes turn toward the shadowy morn;
When bodies weighed with satiate passion's power

shackles:
Fu' to ba' de battle's brunt.
Dey kin fo'ge yo' chains an' shackles
F'om de mountains to de sea;

shanties:
And, supercilious, peer
In shanties by the sides of roads;
And then a quarry pare

shattering:
Our mutable tongue is like the sea,
Curled wave and shattering thunder-fit;
Dangle in strings of sand shall be

shadder:
'Long de lovah's lane;
Frien'ly, shadder-mekin' trees,
'Long de lovah's lane.

sha'n:
Dah, now, I t'ought dat you 'd hug me up close.
Go back, ol' buggah, you sha'n't have dis boy.
He ain't no tramp, ner no straggler, of co'se;

sham:
Because I know it 's true;
Men do not sham convulsion,
Nor simulate a throe.

shadoweth:
Scarcely saved from the gulp of death;
Laid where a willow shadoweth--
Laid where a swelling turf is smooth.

shadily:
'Here the sun shineth
Most shadily;
Here is heard an echo

shakspeare's:
Our five pyramidal acts with one act more,—
We’re lost so! Shakspeare’s ghost could scarcely plead
Against our just damnation. Stand aside;

That’s capable of worship, love, and loss;
We say the same of Shakspeare’s. I’ll be meek,
And learn to reverence, even this poor myself.
